{"name":"Gold Tooth","hex":"#dbb40c","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"gold tooth","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A tooth restoration or decorative cover made from gold or gold alloy.","A removable, decorative piece of jewelry that snaps over the teeth."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gold_tooth"}],"entries":[{"title":"Gold teeth","blocks":[{"text":"Gold teeth are a form of dental prosthesis where the visible part of a tooth is replaced or capped with a prosthetic molded from gold."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Crown_dentistry_gold_03_lateral.jpg/330px-Crown_dentistry_gold_03_lateral.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_teeth","wikidata":"Q2894819"},{"title":"Tooth","blocks":[{"text":"A tooth is a hard, calcified structure found in the jaws of many vertebrates and used to break down food. Some animals, particularly carnivores and omnivores, also use teeth to help with capturing or wounding prey, tearing food, for defensive purposes, to intimidate other animals often including their own, or to carry prey or their young. The roots of teeth are covered by gums. Teeth are not made of bone, but rather of multiple tissues of varying density and hardness that originate from the outermost embryonic germ layer, the ectoderm."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Close_up_-_chimpanzee_teeth.png/330px-Close_up_-_chimpanzee_teeth.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tooth","wikidata":"Q553"},{"title":"Gold","blocks":[{"text":"Gold is a chemical element; its chemical symbol is Au and atomic number 79. In its pure form, it is a bright-metallic-yellow, dense, soft, malleable, and ductile metal. Chemically, gold is a transition metal, a group 11 element, and one of the noble metals. It is one of the least reactive chemical elements, being the second lowest in the reactivity series, with only platinum ranked as less reactive. Gold is solid under standard conditions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Gold-crystals.jpg/330px-Gold-crystals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold","wikidata":"Q897"},{"title":"Gold (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Gold, also called golden, is a color tone resembling the chemical element gold."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg/330px-GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_(color)","wikidata":"Q208045"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Tajikistan_gold_teeth.jpg/500px-Tajikistan_gold_teeth.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Tajikistan_gold_teeth.jpg/500px-Tajikistan_gold_teeth.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A woman with gold teeth from Tajikistan, where they are considered a symbol of wealth","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATajikistan_gold_teeth.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Mammoth_ivory_hg.jpg/500px-Mammoth_ivory_hg.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Mammoth_ivory_hg.jpg/1280px-Mammoth_ivory_hg.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Mammoth_ivory_hg.jpg/500px-Mammoth_ivory_hg.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Section through the ivory tusk of a mammoth","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%